  "worked_solution": "Since $P$ is a polynomial of degree $4,$ the leading term contains an ${x}^{4}.$\n\nSince $Q$ is a polynomial of degree $9,$ the leading term contains an ${x}^{9}.$\n\nIn $P\\cdot Q,$ the ${x}^{4}$ term in $P$ multiplies with the ${x}^{9}$ term in $Q$ to form a term containing ${x}^{4}\\cdot {x}^{9}={x}^{4+9}={x}^{13}.$\n\nTherefore, the degree of $P\\cdot Q$ must be $13.$",
